repl_init — implementation plan
================================

status: done (2026-07-09)

references the shared infrastructure assumptions I1..I5 stated in
plans/repl_list. I2 (named-args encoding) is done (2026-07-09); the
dispatcher entry for this tool already exists:
  "init" => (keys ["repl", "theories"]; Ir.init (get "repl") (get_all "theories"))

why this tool second: it is the first tool with arguments, the only
one with a *list-valued* argument (theories -> repeated keys), and the
gate to every other repl tool — nothing else in wave 1 can be
exercised end-to-end without a created repl. it forces the I3 handler
to convert a json array to repeated pairs, which no other wave-1 tool
needs.

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_init
description   "Create a new REPL proof session that imports the given
              Isabelle theories. This is equivalent to writing
              `theory T imports A B C begin ...` in a .thy file, and
              it is the only way to make a theory's definitions,
              lemmas, and notations available for stepping. Theories
              not in the initial heap must be loaded first with
              load_theory.

              `theories` is a list of theory specs. Examples:
              - [\"Main\"] — start from the standard HOL library
              - [\"HOL-Library.Multiset\"] — import one theory
              - [\"HOL-Library.Multiset\", \"HOL-Library.FSet\"] —
                import and merge multiple theories
              - [\"MySession.MyTheory:42\"] — start from source
                segment 42 of a recorded theory (single spec only)
              - [\"pin@A\"] — start from the pinned state of REPL A
                (use repl_pin first)
              - [\"pin@A\", \"Main\"] — merge a pin with a theory

              The REPL id must be new; remove an old REPL with
              repl_remove first. Discard with repl_remove, inspect
              with repl_list."
              (source: mcp_server.py init at :288 verbatim, with the
              lifecycle sentence adjusted to the repl_* tool names.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "repl": {"type": "string"},
                 "theories": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}},
               "required": ["repl", "theories"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": false,
               "openWorldHint": false}
              (mutating per the spec's blanket, and honestly so: it
              creates a repl; not idempotent — the second identical
              call errors with "REPL ... already exists".)
outputSchema  none (phase 2 policy).

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call name "repl_init",
                    arguments {"repl": "T", "theories": ["HOL.Main"]}
  scala builtin row handler: backend.ir("init",
                    ("repl", repl) :: theories.map("theories" -> _))
                    — the array becomes repeated ("theories", v) pairs
                    IN ORDER (merge order is user-visible).
  bridge            MCP.ir [id, "init", yxml_args]
  ml dispatcher     fname "init" -> Ir.init (get "repl")
                    (get_all "theories")
  engine            ir.ML:393 — parse_spec each string ("Thy",
                    "Thy:idx", "pin@NAME"), resolve, Theory.begin_theory
                    merge (or segment state for the single "Thy:idx"
                    form), insert into repl_tab; out "Created REPL <id>"

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [infra] I1 (MCP_Session.ir) and I3 (builtin table + tools/call
   argument conversion) must exist. this tool adds the array case to
   the I3 conversion: json string values map to one pair, json arrays
   of strings map to repeated pairs in array order; any other json
   shape (numbers arrive elsewhere, objects/null never) -> invalid
   params at the tools/call edge, before the bridge.
2. [this tool] add the builtin row above with handler as in the
   mapping. engine errors (unknown theory, duplicate id, stale pin,
   bad spec) come back as status "error" -> isError tool result.
3. tests below; rebuild per .claude/rules/theories-rebuild.md.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

references I1 (async bridge), I2 (named args — done), I3 (table +
conversion), I5 (output routing).

T1  repeated keys preserve array order through encode/decode/get_all,
    and merge order matters only as Theory.begin_theory order (both
    orders succeed for independent theories).
    test [ml unit]: dispatch init with theories = [Main, Multiset]
    reversed -> both ok; get_all returns encode order (already
    asserted by the I2 round-trip self-test).
T2  empty theories array is rejected by the ENGINE, not the
    dispatcher: keys/get pass (repl present, zero "theories" pairs is
    a valid encoding), Ir.init errors "init requires at least one
    spec" -> status error.
    test [ml unit]: dispatch "init" [("repl", "T")] -> status error
    mentioning "at least one spec"; no repl created (repls output
    unchanged).
T3  duplicate id is an engine error and leaves the existing repl
    untouched.
    test [ml unit]: init "T" [Main]; init "T" [Main] again -> error
    "already exists"; step "T" still works.
T4  an unloaded/unknown theory name is a status error naming the
    theory (Thy_Info.get_theory raises; fork_run captures).
    test [ml unit]: init with theories = ["No.Such_Theory"] -> status
    error; no repl created.
T5  image theories resolve under LONG names — "Main" may fail while
    "HOL.Main" succeeds depending on the heap; the description tells
    the model to check `theories` output. the self-test already
    resolves via suffix match on Thy_Info.get_names().
    test [ml unit]: init with the resolved long name -> ok.
    test [e2e]: tools/call theories, pick a listed name, repl_init
    with it -> ok.
T6  spec forms parse at the engine: "Thy:idx" only as a single spec
    (mixing -> error "Cannot mix theory and segment specs"), "pin@A"
    requires a fresh pin (stale pin / missing pin / busy repl ->
    error).
    test [ml unit]: init ["Main:0"]-style segment spec against a
    record_theories theory -> ok; init ["Main", "Main:0"] -> error;
    init ["pin@nope"] -> error "No REPL".
    (pin round-trip tests live in plans/repl_pin; here only the
    resolve_spec error path.)
T7  the full chain: tools/call repl_init creates a repl visible to
    repl_list.
    test [scala unit]: tools/call "repl_init" {"repl": "T",
    "theories": ["A", "B"]} against Fake_Backend -> fake saw
    ("init", [(repl,T),(theories,A),(theories,B)]).
    test [bridge]: session.ir("init", ...) with HOL.Main -> ok
    "Created REPL"; follow with ir("repls") showing it.
    test [e2e, test_agentic.py]: repl_init -> repl_step "lemma True"
    -> "by simp" -> repl_remove.

